exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
V=[
X(1,'HOW YOU WILL KNOW ME FULLY',
 S('Krishna inviting Arjuna to listen, mind fixed on him','KA','heart','SAMAGRAM'),
 'The Lord said: Hear, Pārtha, how, with your mind attached to me, practising yoga and taking refuge in me, you will know me fully, without doubt.',
 [('mayi āsakta-manāḥ','<b>mind attached to me</b>'),('yogam yuñjan mat-āśrayaḥ','<b>practising yoga, taking refuge in me</b>'),('asaṃśayam samagram mām','<b>without doubt, me in full</b>'),('yathā jñāsyasi tat śṛṇu','<b>how you will know, hear that</b>')],
 'The second six chapters begin. After the yoga of action and meditation, Kṛṣṇa now speaks of himself.',
 'Many commentators divide the Gītā into three groups of six chapters: action (1–6), devotion and the nature of God (7–12), knowledge (13–18). Here the focus shifts to knowing the Lord <i>samagram</i>, fully: in his formless and personal aspects alike.',
 'Knowing someone fully takes time and love. So does knowing the Divine.',
 'Now: <b>knowing the Lord fully</b>.'),
X(2,'KNOWLEDGE AND REALISATION',
 S('A book and a glowing light side by side','K','book','JÑĀNA · VIJÑĀNA'),
 '“I will tell you this knowledge in full, together with realisation; knowing it, nothing more remains to be known here.”',
 [('jñānam te aham sa-vijñānam','<b>knowledge to you, with realisation</b>'),('idam vakṣyāmi aśeṣataḥ','<b>I will tell in full</b>'),('yat jñātvā na iha bhūyaḥ','<b>knowing which, nothing more here</b>'),('anyat jñātavyam avaśiṣyate','<b>remains to be known</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa promises both <i>jñāna</i>, knowledge, and <i>vijñāna</i>, realised knowledge.',
 'Commentators distinguish them: jñāna is understanding; vijñāna is direct experience, or knowledge of the details of the Lord’s manifestation. The chapter’s name, Jñāna Vijñāna Yoga, comes from this pair.',
 'Knowing about something and knowing it from inside are different. Seek both.',
 'Knowledge <b>and realisation</b>.'),
X(3,'ONE IN THOUSANDS',
 S('A thousand people, one climbing a high path, fewer still at the top','','family','ONE IN THOUSANDS'),
 '“Among thousands of people, hardly one strives for perfection; and of those who strive and succeed, hardly one knows me in truth.”',
 [('manuṣyāṇām sahasreṣu','<b>among thousands of people</b>'),('kaścit yatati siddhaye','<b>someone strives for perfection</b>'),('yatatām api siddhānām','<b>of those striving and perfected</b>'),('kaścit mām vetti tattvataḥ','<b>someone knows me in truth</b>')],
 'True knowledge of God is rare.',
 'Not discouragement but a sense of the treasure’s value. Commentators note it is rare not because it is hidden, but because few seek it earnestly.',
 'Rarity is no reason to stop. It means the gift is precious.',
 'True knowing <b>is rare</b>.'),
X(4,'MY LOWER NATURE',
 S('Eight elements around Krishna: earth, water, fire, air, space, mind, intellect, ego','K','guna','8 PARTS'),
 '“Earth, water, fire, air, space, mind, intellect and ego: this is my nature, divided eightfold.”',
 [('bhūmiḥ āpaḥ analaḥ vāyuḥ','<b>earth, water, fire, air</b>'),('kham manaḥ buddhiḥ eva ca','<b>space, mind, intellect</b>'),('ahaṅkāraḥ iti iyam me','<b>and ego: this my</b>'),('bhinnā prakṛtiḥ aṣṭadhā','<b>nature divided eightfold</b>')],
 'The material world, including the mind, is God’s lower nature.',
 'Note that mind, intellect and ego are counted as “matter”, not Self. This echoes the Sāṅkhya system of 24 principles. Commentators say all of them are instruments, not the one who knows.',
 'Even our thoughts and ego belong to nature. The knower behind them is something else.',
 'Matter and mind: <b>my lower nature</b>.'),
X(5,'MY HIGHER NATURE',
 S('A glowing field of life behind the elements','K','light','JĪVA-BHŪTĀ'),
 '“This is the lower. But know my other, higher nature, mighty-armed one: the life-principle (jīva) by which this world is upheld.”',
 [('aparā iyam','<b>this is the lower</b>'),('itaḥ tu anyām prakṛtim','<b>but other than this, a nature</b>'),('viddhi me parām','<b>know my higher one</b>'),('jīva-bhūtām','<b>which is life</b>'),('yayā idam dhāryate jagat','<b>by which this world is upheld</b>')],
 'The higher nature is life itself, the conscious principle.',
 'For Rāmānuja, this is the realm of individual souls (jīvas), part of God’s body. For Śaṅkara, the knower appearing as many through the lower nature. All agree: consciousness is higher than matter.',
 'The world is held up by life. Every living being shares in God’s higher nature.',
 'Life itself is <b>my higher nature</b>.'),
X(6,'ORIGIN AND END OF ALL',
 S('All beings coming from and returning to Krishna','K','wheel','PRABHAVA · PRALAYA'),
 '“Know that all beings arise from these two natures. I am the origin of the whole world and its dissolution.”',
 [('etat-yonīni bhūtāni','<b>beings having these as source</b>'),('sarvāṇi iti upadhāraya','<b>all, understand this</b>'),('aham kṛtsnasya jagataḥ','<b>I, of the whole world</b>'),('prabhavaḥ pralayaḥ tathā','<b>the origin and dissolution</b>')],
 'Matter and life together produce all beings, and the Lord is the source and end of all.',
 'Commentators see God as both the material and efficient cause of the world, like a spider spinning its web from itself (Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad 1.1.7).',
 'Everything comes from one source and returns to it. That makes everything related.',
 'I am <b>the origin and end of all</b>.'),
X(7,'PEARLS ON A THREAD',
 S('A string of pearls with a golden thread running through','K','light','MAṆI-GAṆĀḤ',SKY,''.join(f'<circle cx="{110+k*16}" cy="140" r="7" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(7))+'<path d="M104 140 h110" stroke="#ffd60a" stroke-width="3"/>'),
 '“There is nothing higher than me, Dhanañjaya. All this is strung on me like pearls on a thread.”',
 [('mattaḥ parataram na anyat','<b>nothing higher than me</b>'),('kiñcit asti dhanañjaya','<b>anything exists</b>'),('mayi sarvam idam protam','<b>all this is strung on me</b>'),('sūtre maṇi-gaṇāḥ iva','<b>like pearls on a thread</b>')],
 'One of the Gītā’s loveliest images: God as the hidden thread holding the pearls together.',
 'The thread is unseen, yet without it the necklace falls apart. Commentators of all schools love this verse: God is the unity running through all diversity.',
 'Look for the thread in your life: what holds everything together?',
 'All strung on me <b>like pearls on a thread</b>.'),
X(8,'THE TASTE IN WATER',
 S('Water, the moon and sun, a sacred syllable and a sound','','om','I AM THE TASTE'),
 '“I am the taste in water, son of Kuntī; I am the light in the moon and the sun; I am Oṃ in all the Vedas, the sound in space, and manhood in men.”',
 [('rasaḥ aham apsu','<b>I am the taste in water</b>'),('prabhā asmi śaśi-sūryayoḥ','<b>I am the light in moon and sun</b>'),('praṇavaḥ sarva-vedeṣu','<b>Oṃ in all the Vedas</b>'),('śabdaḥ khe','<b>the sound in space</b>'),('pauruṣam nṛṣu','<b>manhood in men</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa begins to name himself in the essence of things.',
 'The essence of each thing: taste in water, light in sun and moon, Oṃ in the Vedas. Commentators say this is a meditation: see the Divine in the best quality of everything.',
 'When you drink water, taste it, and remember this verse.',
 'I am <b>the taste in water</b>.'),
X(9,'FRAGRANCE AND FIRE',
 S('Fragrant earth, a bright fire, an ascetic glowing','','fire','PUṆYO GANDHAḤ'),
 '“I am the pure fragrance of the earth and the brilliance in fire; I am the life in all beings and the austerity in ascetics.”',
 [('puṇyaḥ gandhaḥ pṛthivyām ca','<b>the pure fragrance in earth</b>'),('tejaḥ ca asmi vibhāvasau','<b>the brilliance in fire</b>'),('jīvanam sarva-bhūteṣu','<b>the life in all beings</b>'),('tapaḥ ca asmi tapasviṣu','<b>austerity in ascetics</b>')],
 'More essences: the fragrance of earth, the brightness of fire.',
 'The smell of rain on dry earth is the Divine, says this verse. Commentators note that each element is named with its subtle quality: earth with smell, fire with light.',
 'Notice the smell of earth after rain. That too is a doorway.',
 'I am <b>the fragrance of the earth</b>.'),
X(10,'THE ETERNAL SEED',
 S('A seed from which all beings sprout','','seed','SANĀTANA BĪJA'),
 '“Know me, Pārtha, as the eternal seed of all beings. I am the intelligence of the intelligent and the splendour of the splendid.”',
 [('bījam mām sarva-bhūtānām','<b>me as the seed of all beings</b>'),('viddhi pārtha sanātanam','<b>know, eternal</b>'),('buddhiḥ buddhimatām asmi','<b>I am the intelligence of the intelligent</b>'),('tejaḥ tejasvinām aham','<b>the splendour of the splendid</b>')],
 'God as the seed of all, and the best quality in each.',
 'A seed holds the whole tree. Commentators say God is present in every being as its potential and its best gift.',
 'Your intelligence and gifts are not just yours. Honour their source.',
 'I am <b>the eternal seed</b>.'),
X(11,'STRENGTH WITHOUT DESIRE',
 S('A strong man helping others, desire within limits of dharma','P','heart','DHARMA-AVIRUDDHA'),
 '“I am the strength of the strong, free from desire and passion; and in beings I am desire that is not against dharma, best of the Bharatas.”',
 [('balam balavatām ca aham','<b>I am the strength of the strong</b>'),('kāma-rāga-vivarjitam','<b>free from desire and passion</b>'),('dharma-aviruddhaḥ bhūteṣu','<b>not against dharma, in beings</b>'),('kāmaḥ asmi','<b>I am desire</b>')],
 'Strength without selfishness, and desire in harmony with dharma, are divine.',
 'A striking verse: desire is not condemned outright. Desire within dharma, such as wanting a family or honest prosperity, is a form of the Divine. Commentators note the balance with 3.37.',
 'Not all desire is bad. Desire within right limits is sacred.',
 'Desire within dharma <b>is divine</b>.'),
X(12,'THE THREE GUṆAS ARE FROM ME',
 S('Three coloured spheres flowing from Krishna, who stands beyond them','K','guna','NOT IN THEM'),
 '“Whatever states there are, of sattva, rajas or tamas, know that they come from me. But I am not in them; they are in me.”',
 [('ye ca eva sāttvikāḥ bhāvāḥ','<b>whatever sāttvika states</b>'),('rājasāḥ tāmasāḥ ca ye','<b>rājasika and tāmasika</b>'),('mattaḥ eva iti tān viddhi','<b>know them as from me alone</b>'),('na tu aham teṣu te mayi','<b>but I am not in them; they are in me</b>')],
 'All three guṇas come from God, but God is not bound by them.',
 'A subtle point: the world depends on God; God does not depend on the world. Commentators compare it to the sky holding clouds without being touched.',
 'The source is untouched by what flows from it. So, at our core, are we.',
 'They are in me; <b>I am not in them</b>.'),
X(13,'DELUDED BY THE GUṆAS',
 S('People caught in a three-coloured haze, a light beyond them unseen','','guna','CAN’T SEE'),
 '“The whole world, deluded by these states of the three guṇas, does not know me, who am beyond them and imperishable.”',
 [('tribhiḥ guṇa-mayaiḥ bhāvaiḥ','<b>by the three guṇa-states</b>'),('ebhiḥ sarvam idam jagat','<b>this whole world</b>'),('mohitam na abhijānāti','<b>deluded, does not know</b>'),('mām ebhyaḥ param avyayam','<b>me, beyond them, imperishable</b>')],
 'Absorbed in the play of the guṇas, the world misses its source.',
 'Commentators: we are so busy with moods and events that we miss the one who is behind them.',
 'Look past moods and events, toward what never changes.',
 'Caught in the guṇas, <b>the world misses me</b>.'),
X(14,'MY DIVINE MĀYĀ',
 S('A shimmering veil, a person holding Krishna’s hand passing through','KA','light','MĀYĀ'),
 '“This divine māyā of mine, made of the guṇas, is hard to cross. But those who take refuge in me alone cross over it.”',
 [('daivī hi eṣā guṇa-mayī','<b>this divine one, made of guṇas</b>'),('mama māyā duratyayā','<b>my māyā, hard to cross</b>'),('mām eva ye prapadyante','<b>those who take refuge in me alone</b>'),('māyām etām taranti te','<b>cross over this māyā</b>')],
 'The way across illusion: surrender to the Lord.',
 'A central verse for devotion. <i>Prapatti</i>, surrender, is the method. Rāmānuja’s school builds much on it. Śaṅkara reads refuge as knowledge of the Self. All agree māyā cannot be crossed by effort alone.',
 'Some knots cannot be untied by our own hands. Asking for grace is wisdom, not weakness.',
 'Take refuge in me, <b>and cross māyā</b>.'),
X(15,'THOSE WHO DO NOT COME',
 S('People turning away from a light, robbed by a shadow of illusion','','demon','TURNED AWAY'),
 '“Evil-doers, the deluded, the lowest of people, whose knowledge is stolen by māyā and who take on a demonic nature, do not take refuge in me.”',
 [('na mām duṣkṛtinaḥ mūḍhāḥ','<b>evil-doers, the deluded do not (seek) me</b>'),('prapadyante nara-adhamāḥ','<b>the lowest of people do not surrender</b>'),('māyayā apahṛta-jñānāḥ','<b>knowledge stolen by māyā</b>'),('āsuram bhāvam āśritāḥ','<b>taking on a demonic nature</b>')],
 'Four kinds who turn away.',
 'The “demonic nature” is described fully in chapter 16. Commentators note it is a state, not a fixed category: anyone can change.',
 'Turning away is a state of mind, not a fate. It can change.',
 'Some <b>turn away</b>.'),
X(16,'FOUR KINDS OF DEVOTEES',
 S('Four devotees: one in distress, one curious, one wanting, one wise','','family','4 KINDS'),
 '“Four kinds of virtuous people worship me, Arjuna: the distressed, the seeker of knowledge, the seeker of wealth, and the wise, best of the Bharatas.”',
 [('catur-vidhāḥ bhajante mām','<b>four kinds worship me</b>'),('janāḥ sukṛtinaḥ','<b>virtuous people</b>'),('ārtaḥ','<b>the distressed</b>'),('jijñāsuḥ','<b>the seeker of knowledge</b>'),('artha-arthī','<b>the seeker of wealth</b>'),('jñānī','<b>the wise</b>')],
 'Four reasons people come to God.',
 'Commentators give classic examples: Draupadī in distress, Uddhava the seeker, Dhruva wanting a kingdom, Śuka the wise. All four are called <i>sukṛtin</i>, virtuous: turning to God for any reason is good.',
 'Whatever brings you to prayer, distress, curiosity or need, it is a good start.',
 'Four doors <b>to the Lord</b>.'),
X(17,'THE WISE ONE IS DEAREST',
 S('A wise devotee and Krishna embracing','KA','heart','PRIYAḤ'),
 '“Of these, the wise one, ever united, devoted to the One, excels. For I am exceedingly dear to the wise, and he is dear to me.”',
 [('teṣām jñānī nitya-yuktaḥ','<b>of them the wise, ever united</b>'),('eka-bhaktiḥ viśiṣyate','<b>single in devotion, excels</b>'),('priyaḥ hi jñāninaḥ atyartham','<b>I am very dear to the wise</b>'),('aham saḥ ca mama priyaḥ','<b>and he is dear to me</b>')],
 'The wise devotee, who loves God for God’s own sake, is the best.',
 '<i>Eka-bhakti</i>, devotion to one alone, wanting nothing else. Commentators say the others love God for something; the wise love God himself.',
 'Love that wants nothing in return is the purest.',
 'The wise devotee <b>is dearest</b>.'),
X(18,'ALL ARE NOBLE',
 S('All four devotees welcomed, the wise one nearest','K','family','UDĀRĀḤ'),
 '“All these are noble, but the wise one I regard as my very Self. For, united, he abides in me alone as the highest goal.”',
 [('udārāḥ sarve eva ete','<b>all these are noble</b>'),('jñānī tu ātmā eva me matam','<b>but the wise is my very Self, I hold</b>'),('āsthitaḥ saḥ hi yukta-ātmā','<b>for, united, he abides</b>'),('mām eva anuttamām gatim','<b>in me as the highest goal</b>')],
 'All are welcome; the wise is one with God.',
 'Commentators of Advaita read “my very Self” literally; Rāmānuja reads it as “as dear as my own life”. Either way, Kṛṣṇa calls every seeker <i>udāra</i>, generous, noble.',
 'Don’t look down on anyone’s reasons for seeking. All are noble.',
 'All are noble; <b>the wise is my Self</b>.'),
X(19,'VĀSUDEVA IS ALL',
 S('A wise person seeing Krishna in everything around him','Y','vishnu','VĀSUDEVAḤ SARVAM'),
 '“At the end of many births the wise one takes refuge in me, knowing ‘Vāsudeva is all.’ Such a great soul is very rare.”',
 [('bahūnām janmanām ante','<b>at the end of many births</b>'),('jñānavān mām prapadyate','<b>the wise one surrenders to me</b>'),('vāsudevaḥ sarvam iti','<b>“Vāsudeva is all”</b>'),('saḥ mahā-ātmā su-durlabhaḥ','<b>that great soul is very rare</b>')],
 'A famous line: <i>vāsudevaḥ sarvam</i>, God is everything.',
 'This verse is treasured across traditions. It is the fruit of a long journey over many lives. Commentators call such a person a true <i>mahātmā</i>, great soul.',
 'Seeing the Divine in everything is the goal of a long journey. Every step counts.',
 '<b>“Vāsudeva is all”</b>: very rare.'),
X(20,'OTHER GODS FOR OTHER WISHES',
 S('People at shrines of many gods, each holding a wish','','gods','OTHER GODS'),
 '“Those whose knowledge is stolen by various desires turn to other gods, following various rites, constrained by their own nature.”',
 [('kāmaiḥ taiḥ taiḥ hṛta-jñānāḥ','<b>knowledge stolen by various desires</b>'),('prapadyante anya-devatāḥ','<b>turn to other gods</b>'),('tam tam niyamam āsthāya','<b>following this and that rule</b>'),('prakṛtyā niyatāḥ svayā','<b>bound by their own nature</b>')],
 'People worship various gods for various wishes, according to their nature.',
 'Not a condemnation of worship of other deities, but an observation: desire-driven worship stays within desire. Commentators connect it to 4.11–12.',
 'Ask what you pray for. The answer shows what you value.',
 'Desires lead to <b>many altars</b>.'),
X(21,'I MAKE THEIR FAITH STEADY',
 S('Krishna quietly strengthening the faith of a worshipper at another shrine','K','heart','I STEADY IT'),
 '“Whatever form any devotee wishes to worship with faith, I make that very faith of his unwavering.”',
 [('yaḥ yaḥ yām yām tanum bhaktaḥ','<b>whatever form, whatever devotee</b>'),('śraddhayā arcitum icchati','<b>wishes to worship with faith</b>'),('tasya tasya acalām śraddhām','<b>his faith, unwavering</b>'),('tām eva vidadhāmi aham','<b>that very one I make</b>')],
 'A generous verse: God supports every sincere faith.',
 'Commentators see God behind every sincere worship, even when the worshipper does not know it. It is one of the Gītā’s most broad-minded statements.',
 'Respect others’ faith. The same Lord may be steadying it.',
 'I make <b>every sincere faith firm</b>.'),
X(22,'THE GIFTS COME FROM ME',
 S('A worshipper receiving gifts that secretly come from Krishna’s hand','K','gift','FROM ME'),
 '“Endowed with that faith, he worships that form and gains his desires from it; but those are granted by me alone.”',
 [('saḥ tayā śraddhayā yuktaḥ','<b>he, endowed with that faith</b>'),('tasya ārādhanam īhate','<b>seeks its worship</b>'),('labhate ca tataḥ kāmān','<b>and gains desires from it</b>'),('mayā eva vihitān hi tān','<b>but they are granted by me alone</b>')],
 'Whatever god one worships, it is the one Lord who answers.',
 'Commentators say all divine powers are expressions of one Supreme; blessings flow from that source.',
 'Gratitude for any blessing can lead back to the one source.',
 'All gifts come <b>from one source</b>.'),
X(23,'LIMITED FRUIT',
 S('A small fruit compared to a great tree of light','','seed','ANTAVAT'),
 '“But the fruit for those of small understanding is limited. Worshippers of the gods go to the gods; my devotees come to me.”',
 [('antavat tu phalam teṣām','<b>but their fruit has an end</b>'),('tat bhavati alpa-medhasām','<b>for those of small understanding</b>'),('devān deva-yajaḥ yānti','<b>god-worshippers go to the gods</b>'),('mat-bhaktāḥ yānti mām api','<b>my devotees come to me</b>')],
 'Worship for limited ends gives limited results.',
 'The goal shapes the result: lesser goals, lesser fruits. Commentators note that even the gods’ heavens are temporary.',
 'Aim high. What you seek is what you find.',
 'Small aims, <b>small fruit</b>.'),
X(24,'THEY THINK I AM MERELY HUMAN',
 S('People seeing only a human Krishna, missing his glow','K','light','MORE THAN HE SEEMS'),
 '“The unwise think of me, the unmanifest, as having become manifest, not knowing my higher nature, imperishable and supreme.”',
 [('avyaktam vyaktim āpannam','<b>the unmanifest as become manifest</b>'),('manyante mām abuddhayaḥ','<b>the unwise think me</b>'),('param bhāvam ajānantaḥ','<b>not knowing my higher nature</b>'),('mama avyayam anuttamam','<b>imperishable, supreme</b>')],
 'Many see only the human Kṛṣṇa, not the divine.',
 'Commentators: seeing Kṛṣṇa as a mere man is the error; his form is divine. Śaṅkara’s and Rāmānuja’s readings differ on the nature of that form, but both reject the “merely human” view.',
 'People are often more than they seem. So is the world.',
 'More than <b>a mere man</b>.'),
X(25,'HIDDEN BY YOGA-MĀYĀ',
 S('A veil covering Krishna from a crowd','K','light','VEILED'),
 '“I am not revealed to all, veiled by my yoga-māyā. This deluded world does not know me, the unborn, the imperishable.”',
 [('na aham prakāśaḥ sarvasya','<b>I am not manifest to all</b>'),('yoga-māyā-samāvṛtaḥ','<b>veiled by yoga-māyā</b>'),('mūḍhaḥ ayam na abhijānāti','<b>this deluded one does not know</b>'),('lokaḥ mām ajam avyayam','<b>the world, me, unborn, imperishable</b>')],
 'God hides by his own power.',
 '<i>Yoga-māyā</i> is the Lord’s power to veil himself. Commentators say the veil lifts for those who seek with love.',
 'What is hidden can be found by those who look sincerely.',
 'Veiled <b>by my own power</b>.'),
X(26,'I KNOW ALL TIMES',
 S('Krishna seeing past, present and future beings, unseen by them','K','star','I KNOW ALL'),
 '“I know the beings of the past, the present and the future, Arjuna; but no one knows me.”',
 [('veda aham samatītāni','<b>I know those past</b>'),('vartamānāni ca arjuna','<b>and present</b>'),('bhaviṣyāṇi ca bhūtāni','<b>and future beings</b>'),('mām tu veda na kaścana','<b>but no one knows me</b>')],
 'God knows all; few know God.',
 'Commentators say “no one” means none without his grace. The asymmetry humbles the seeker.',
 'We are fully known. That can be a comfort.',
 'I know all; <b>few know me</b>.'),
X(27,'BORN INTO DELUSION',
 S('Beings born into a haze of likes and dislikes','','storm','DVANDVA-MOHA'),
 '“By the delusion of the pairs of opposites arising from desire and aversion, Bhārata, all beings fall into delusion at birth.”',
 [('icchā-dveṣa-samutthena','<b>arising from desire and aversion</b>'),('dvandva-mohena bhārata','<b>by the delusion of opposites</b>'),('sarva-bhūtāni sammoham','<b>all beings into delusion</b>'),('sarge yānti','<b>go at birth</b>')],
 'Why we don’t see: likes and dislikes cloud us from birth.',
 'Commentators: the pairs of opposites are the basic programme of every creature. Freedom means seeing past them.',
 'Notice your likes and dislikes. They are not the whole truth.',
 'Born <b>into the fog of opposites</b>.'),
X(28,'THOSE WHOSE SIN HAS ENDED',
 S('Virtuous people stepping out of the fog toward Krishna','P','light','DṚḌHA-VRATĀḤ'),
 '“But people of virtuous deeds, whose sin has come to an end, freed from the delusion of opposites, worship me with firm resolve.”',
 [('yeṣām tu anta-gatam pāpam','<b>but those whose sin has ended</b>'),('janānām puṇya-karmaṇām','<b>people of virtuous deeds</b>'),('te dvandva-moha-nirmuktāḥ','<b>freed from the delusion of opposites</b>'),('bhajante mām dṛḍha-vratāḥ','<b>worship me with firm vows</b>')],
 'Good deeds clear the way to steady devotion.',
 'Commentators: karma yoga purifies; purity frees one from opposites; then devotion becomes firm.',
 'Good deeds prepare the heart. Keep doing them.',
 'Good deeds <b>clear the way</b>.'),
X(29,'TO BE FREE OF AGE AND DEATH',
 S('People taking refuge in Krishna to be free of old age and death','KA','light','JARĀ-MARAṆA'),
 '“Those who take refuge in me and strive for freedom from old age and death know Brahman fully, the whole of the inner Self, and all action.”',
 [('jarā-maraṇa-mokṣāya','<b>for freedom from old age and death</b>'),('mām āśritya yatanti ye','<b>who, relying on me, strive</b>'),('te brahma tat viduḥ kṛtsnam','<b>they know that Brahman fully</b>'),('adhyātmam karma ca akhilam','<b>the inner Self and all action</b>')],
 'Seekers of freedom come to know Brahman, the Self and action.',
 'This verse introduces terms (Brahman, adhyātma, karma) that Arjuna will ask about at the start of chapter 8.',
 'The deepest questions lead to the deepest answers. Keep asking.',
 'Seeking freedom, <b>they know all</b>.'),
X(30,'EVEN AT THE TIME OF DEATH',
 S('A dying person peacefully remembering Krishna','KA','om','PRAYĀṆA-KĀLE'),
 '“Those who know me as the ground of beings, of the gods and of sacrifice, even at the time of death, with minds united, know me.”',
 [('sa-adhibhūta-adhidaivam mām','<b>me with adhibhūta and adhidaiva</b>'),('sa-adhiyajñam ca ye viduḥ','<b>and with adhiyajña, who know</b>'),('prayāṇa-kāle api ca mām','<b>even at the time of departure, me</b>'),('te viduḥ yukta-cetasaḥ','<b>they know, with united minds</b>')],
 'Those who know God in all these aspects remember him even at death.',
 'Three more terms: adhibhūta, adhidaiva, adhiyajña. Arjuna asks about all of them in 8.1–2. The chapter ends on the moment of death, the theme of chapter 8.',
 'What we cultivate in life is what remains at the end.',
 'Known in life, <b>remembered at death</b>.'),
]
